WEBVTT

00:00:00.567 --> 00:00:18.911
<v Casey>Hey there, welcome in. On today's episode: pristine asteroid dust from Bennu carrying the sugar in RNA, a physics result that bends a century-old textbook rule, and shades that helped bleaching coral. Plus our Deep Dive question: can a handful of volunteers with thermometers produce knowledge worth acting on? Stay with us, it's coming right up.

00:00:32.386 --> 00:00:52.498
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Casey. It's Sunday, October 4th, 2026. Today's theme is Science, Wonder &amp; the Natural World. We're speaking to you from the traditional territories of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations here in the Cariboo region.

00:00:53.498 --> 00:01:25.122
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. Today's episode runs from coral reefs to asteroid dust to a physics result with a hundred years of history behind it. Weather first. Out at Horsefly Lake it's overcast and about 5 degrees this morning, with a high of 21 and a low of 4 today. Light drizzle, though only a 4 percent chance of any measurable rain. 100 Mile House is around 6 with clear skies, Williams Lake around 6 and overcast, Quesnel around 6 and mainly clear, and Alexis Creek around 4 and overcast.

00:01:25.577 --> 00:01:28.329
<v Casey>Good day to be indoors with a kettle and the news.

00:01:29.729 --> 00:01:37.009
<v Riley>A lot of this morning's news sits close to home, with the provincial campaign in full swing. After that the science desk has been busy.

00:01:38.777 --> 00:02:09.265
<v Riley>Williams Lake first. The Orange Shirt Society hosted a community breakfast on September 30th, and the Williams Lake Tribune's Rebecca Dyok reports on the day's focus: hearing truth and honouring survivors. The gathering put survivors' accounts at the centre of the morning, with people speaking to a room that had come to listen. For a region where residential school history is a living family history, the breakfast is a reminder that the listening part of reconciliation has no deadline.

00:02:10.692 --> 00:03:22.728
<v Casey>On the campaign trail, B.C.'s three major party leaders pitched plans Friday on wildfire response, grocery affordability and artificial intelligence regulation. My Cariboo Now reports the Conservatives pledged four hundred million dollars to buy water bombers. The province votes October 24th. In Cariboo-Chilcotin, Lorne Doerkson is the Conservative incumbent. The Tribune calls him the party's interim leader, and the 100 Mile Free Press reported in late September that he took the helm days before the snap election was called. Elections BC results show he was first elected in 2020 as a B.C. Liberal and re-elected in 2024 as a Conservative. In 2024 My Cariboo Now's partial count had him ahead of the NDP's Michael Moses, roughly 69 per cent to 30. The NDP candidate this time is Kathryn Askew, a retired teacher from Canim Lake with 32 years in education, per the Tribune on October 1st. She says she's running because she believes in David Eby's leadership. In Prince George-North Cariboo, which includes Quesnel, Sheldon Clare won the seat for the Conservatives in 2024, and Elections BC's candidate list shows him as the Conservative filing again. The show hasn't established the other names in that race yet.

00:03:23.178 --> 00:03:25.570
<v Riley>Water bombers are a long-lead purchase, though.

00:03:27.003 --> 00:03:48.699
<v Casey>The 100 Mile Free Press reports the first CariBOO Con, held September 18th to 20th, raised over a thousand dollars for the 108 Heritage Site. A first-year convention clearing four figures for a heritage site is a decent start. Heritage sites in small communities usually run on a few donors and a few volunteers, so a new event with its own audience matters.

00:03:50.147 --> 00:04:28.609
<v Riley>Nature funding next. The Tyee reported that environmental groups say Prime Minister Carney's push to fund industry puts Canada's nature promises at risk. Nature Canada's executive director, Emily McMillan, says the group celebrated the government's $3.8 billion investment this spring. She is puzzled that the same government now floats proposals that could create what she calls "zones of environmental lawlessness." For the Cariboo, where mining, forestry and grassland conservation all sit in one watershed, those exemptions matter. A species-at-risk survey means little if the land it covers can be fast-tracked past review.

00:04:30.213 --> 00:04:53.885
<v Casey>Reef science now. Researchers in the Florida Keys put 20 temporary underwater shades over brain coral colonies from August to November 2024. During a stretch of high sea temperatures, shaded corals bleached less and some regained colour in two Atlantic species. Shading a reef is a rescue tactic that doesn't scale easily, since every shade is a physical structure someone has to place and take down.

00:04:55.385 --> 00:05:15.601
<v Riley>ScienceAlert reported on a study suggesting even very slow running benefits the brain. The researchers looked at how brain measures changed in people who ran at gentle speeds, and the benefits didn't require hard effort. That's good news if your trail is gravel and your hill is steep. A slow jog down a logging road counts.

00:05:17.164 --> 00:05:37.572
<v Casey>Brain scans have turned up evidence for a possible cause of long COVID fatigue and brain fog. Researchers report signs that long COVID may injure dopamine-releasing neurons. That could explain the fatigue, low motivation, slowed movement and memory trouble people describe. It's early, and it's imaging rather than a treatment, but it points to a biological target.

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:53.318
<v Riley>Related territory. Neuroscience News reports on a new study aiming to investigate how chronic migraine weakens brain blood vessels, alters the brain's waste-clearing system and raises stroke risk. It's a research programme, not a result.

00:05:54.753 --> 00:06:13.801
<v Casey>STAT News reports a Maui study that found residents carrying high levels of toxic metals months after the wildfire. The authors argue post-fire air quality needs monitoring over the long term, not just while the smoke is visible. Anyone who has lived through a Cariboo summer of smoke knows the monitoring usually stops when the headlines do.

00:06:15.296 --> 00:06:28.632
<v Riley>A TechRadar report on wave energy. Researchers propose fields of house-sized buoys that could capture up to 90 percent of wave energy, with each buoy powering several hundred households. It's theoretical for now.

00:06:30.125 --> 00:06:44.309
<v Casey>The Verge covered a cut cable that disrupted hundreds of flights across the US. Construction crews in New Jersey accidentally cut a Verizon fiber line used for air traffic control. One backhoe, one cable, and hundreds of flights were cancelled or delayed.

00:06:44.759 --> 00:06:46.511
<v Riley>Rural folks have known that for years.

00:06:47.989 --> 00:07:01.349
<v Casey>ScienceDaily reports that quantum fluctuations in supposedly empty space can strengthen superconductivity. The transition temperature of an ultrathin material rose by as much as 5.4 percent. Empty space apparently has opinions.

00:07:02.918 --> 00:07:15.758
<v Riley>And the Large Hadron Collider search for microscopic quantum black holes came up empty. Physicists found no evidence the machine has been producing any. It sharply narrows where that exotic physics could still be hiding.

00:07:16.208 --> 00:07:23.432
<v Casey>Which leads into this morning's Deep Dive, where we're asking what small samples can actually tell you. First, a local organization.

00:07:24.832 --> 00:07:48.784
<v Riley>The South Cariboo Sustainability Society has been working out of 100 Mile House since 2008. They run community gardens, host an environmental film series, and offer education on waste reduction and ecological resilience. It's the kind of group that does the slow, practical work of helping a town live within its own ecosystem, and it has stuck at it for close to two decades.

00:07:49.088 --> 00:07:52.976
<v Casey>Two decades of community gardens is a long-term dataset in its own way.

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:56.044
<v Riley>Good bridge, because that's where the Deep Dive starts.

00:07:57.444 --> 00:08:12.308
<v Casey>The whole region loves small. Small mills, small farms, small classrooms. So take a small citizen-science project, say a handful of volunteers logging water temperature or bird arrivals around Horsefly or Williams Lake. Is that real, usable knowledge, or too thin to act on?

00:08:12.639 --> 00:08:38.287
<v Riley>Usable, if it's designed right. Look at the Bennu sample. NASA's OSIRIS-REx mission brought back a small amount of pristine asteroid material, and researchers have reportedly found ribose in it, the sugar in RNA, along with glucose and other sugars. Ribose turned up in meteorites like Murchison before, but those had sat on Earth where contamination is a live question. A small clean sample beat a big dirty one.

00:08:38.839 --> 00:08:54.023
<v Casey>Pristine and tiny, sure. But Bennu has the full RNA ingredient set, sugar, phosphate and nucleobases in one body, and ingredients aren't a pathway to life. Deoxyribose, the DNA sugar, was absent. That's suggestive for the RNA-first idea. It's not proof, and it's one asteroid.

00:08:54.658 --> 00:09:10.970
<v Riley>Nobody's claiming proof. The claim is that the ingredients were available. The sugars are thought to have formed in the asteroid's brines, through water altering the rock. Ryugu samples haven't been checked for sugars yet, so a second sample is coming. Small samples get a second look.

00:09:11.649 --> 00:09:19.417
<v Casey>Bennu got a billion-dollar mission and teams of chemists to keep it clean. A volunteer with a thermometer on the Horsefly River doesn't.

00:09:19.957 --> 00:09:24.805
<v Riley>The volunteer's data doesn't have to be pristine to be useful. It has to be consistent.

00:09:25.357 --> 00:09:31.269
<v Casey>Consistent for how long? Volunteers move, retire, get bored. Small projects tend to end when one person does.

00:09:31.952 --> 00:09:38.984
<v Riley>One season is a pilot. Then you automate the boring part. Loggers clipped to a bridge piling run for months with nobody there.

00:09:39.291 --> 00:09:41.171
<v Casey>And who downloads the card in year five?

00:09:41.513 --> 00:09:43.409
<v Riley>Someone with a procedure written down.

00:09:43.691 --> 00:09:47.179
<v Casey>Write-ups are what get lost, in my experience. A binder in a truck.

00:09:47.079 --> 00:09:48.127
<v Riley>Then put it online.

00:09:48.418 --> 00:09:50.650
<v Casey>Fine. Online. And then does anybody trust it?

00:09:50.906 --> 00:10:20.690
<v Riley>The Hall effect study shows how small can still be credible. Carnegie Mellon researchers published in Nature Materials about an in-plane anomalous Hall effect. For a century the assumption was you need a magnetic field perpendicular to the material to see the effect. In this nanoscale device, a heterostructure of two layered materials, magnetization within the plane produced a detectable electrical response. A tiny device, a single interface, and it forces a rethink of the textbook picture.

00:10:21.307 --> 00:10:42.611
<v Casey>The ScienceAlert headline says the effect "doesn't work how we thought it did." The finding is that the effect shows up in an exotic material with reduced symmetry. That extends it. It doesn't overturn Hall. And the experiments ran at cryogenic temperatures, so there's no room-temperature result yet. A nanoscale device in a very cold lab is a long way from a sensor in a pickup.

00:10:43.126 --> 00:10:56.830
<v Riley>The payoff is potentially one ultra-thin sensor reading magnetic fields along multiple axes. That could shrink sensors in electronics, transport and medical imaging. The path from lab to product is long, but the direction is clear.

00:10:57.364 --> 00:11:06.172
<v Casey>The headline ran ahead of the physics, and local monitoring does the same thing. A few good readings get turned into "river is warming" by people who need a story.

00:11:06.714 --> 00:11:17.538
<v Riley>Same standard for both. Claim what the data supports. A volunteer temperature log supports "this stretch ran warm in August." It doesn't support a causal story, and nobody needs it to.

00:11:18.136 --> 00:11:26.112
<v Casey>Only if the claim gets scoped that carefully every time. It won't be. A council report will round it up, a funding pitch will round it up again.

00:11:26.635 --> 00:11:35.683
<v Riley>Then the protocol has to say what the data can't show. One paragraph, attached to every release. Medical studies do this. Limitations section, same page.

00:11:36.304 --> 00:11:42.328
<v Casey>Medical studies also get misread by the headline writers. The limitations section exists and nobody reads it.

00:11:42.838 --> 00:11:48.214
<v Riley>Some do. The people who fund the next season do, and they're the ones who matter for a small project.

00:11:48.394 --> 00:11:48.818
<v Casey>Hm. Maybe.

00:11:49.217 --> 00:12:17.425
<v Riley>The Google quantum story shows the secrecy angle. As Scientific American tells it, Google Quantum AI published a zero-knowledge proof in March showing optimized quantum circuits could break elliptic-curve cryptography, the kind used by Bitcoin and Ethereum. The actual method stayed withheld, reportedly with U.S. government involvement. A zero-knowledge proof lets you prove a claim without handing over the blueprint. For dual-use science that's a useful new norm.

00:12:18.094 --> 00:12:41.830
<v Casey>It backfired. Trail of Bits reportedly found memory-safety and logic bugs in the prover code and forged a proof with absurdly low resource claims. A cryptologist, André Schrottenloher, reportedly rebuilt the circuit in 63 days from public literature and trimmed the gate count. Then an AI-assisted community effort reportedly cut the Toffoli gate count by nearly half within days. The secret got rebuilt in the open.

00:12:42.108 --> 00:12:45.500
<v Riley>Which shows open replication works fast when people care.

00:12:46.015 --> 00:12:56.015
<v Casey>And that a closed team with one prover implementation was the weak point. A zero-knowledge proof removes the need to see the method. You still have to trust whoever wrote the code.

00:12:56.604 --> 00:13:06.284
<v Riley>So give the citizen scientist the open version of that. Publish the raw readings, the sensor model, the placement, the date. Anyone can check. Small, but checkable.

00:13:06.895 --> 00:13:11.295
<v Casey>Checkable only if somebody checks. Plenty of open datasets sit untouched for years.

00:13:11.932 --> 00:13:19.188
<v Riley>Some do. In this case Schrottenloher did it in 63 days with no insider access. Interest follows interesting results.

00:13:19.828 --> 00:13:24.348
<v Casey>A circuit that could break Bitcoin's cryptography is more interesting than a creek thermometer.

00:13:24.929 --> 00:13:30.105
<v Riley>Ha. True. Nobody's writing the breathless headline about Horsefly River water at 14 degrees.

00:13:30.285 --> 00:13:31.325
<v Casey>Unless it's 19.

00:13:31.725 --> 00:13:32.749
<v Riley>Then somebody checks.

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:50.448
<v Casey>There's a second question in that story. Who decides what's too dangerous to publish? Google and a government agency made that call for a quantum result. In the Cariboo the equivalent is smaller and stranger. Who decides whether the location of a spawning bed or a rare plant gets posted on a public dataset?

00:13:50.783 --> 00:14:03.183
<v Riley>The open-data answer has a cost. Poachers read maps too. Species-at-risk programs often blur coordinates to a grid square for that reason. The data stays useful for trends and useless for a trophy hunter.

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:11.642
<v Casey>Blurred by whom, and who holds the original? That's the same trust problem as the prover code, shrunk down to a volunteer with a spreadsheet.

00:14:12.203 --> 00:14:17.075
<v Riley>It's solvable with a data-sharing agreement at the start. Which is dull, and it works.

00:14:17.674 --> 00:14:41.618
<v Casey>The Cariboo has something the physics labs don't. Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in and Dakelh communities have watched these rivers and forests across generations. That's long-baseline observation, and it's anything but thin. If a volunteer temperature logger gets set up without those relationships, you get a new small dataset sitting next to an old, much larger body of knowledge it never asked about. Outreach should come before the sensor.

00:14:42.221 --> 00:14:50.917
<v Riley>And the technology can serve it. A logger gives numbers that sit alongside long observation and lets people compare. It doesn't replace anything.

00:14:51.475 --> 00:14:57.611
<v Casey>If the community wants that and controls where the numbers go. I'd rather hear that from the community than assume it.

00:14:57.906 --> 00:15:00.082
<v Riley>The relationship comes first. Agreed.

00:15:00.733 --> 00:15:16.541
<v Casey>The Maui study shows another failure of small. Post-fire toxic metals lingered for months, and the case there is that monitoring should run longer than the smoke. Plenty of programs track the visible event and stop. A small monitor that runs continuously beats a large one that switches off.

00:15:17.144 --> 00:15:27.448
<v Riley>So persistence is the advantage a small local program can have. A federal team shows up for the event. People who live in Williams Lake or Quesnel are still here in March.

00:15:27.731 --> 00:15:31.715
<v Casey>If they're still funded in March, and still there. A retirement is all it takes.

00:15:32.333 --> 00:15:48.813
<v Riley>The Cariboo has teachers, retired biologists, a college. A project run through an institution that outlasts any one person has a better shot. A school science club logging the same creek every autumn has a succession plan built in, because kids graduate and new ones arrive.

00:15:49.361 --> 00:15:57.769
<v Casey>Better than a lone enthusiast. Though a school club depends on a teacher who cares, and it reads to me as the same retirement problem with more paperwork.

00:15:58.124 --> 00:16:00.244
<v Riley>More paperwork is a form of succession.

00:16:00.585 --> 00:16:01.865
<v Casey>Ha. I'll give you that one.

00:16:02.501 --> 00:16:15.501
<v Riley>And the Tyee story ties in. If environmental review gets loosened, a local baseline is the only record of what was there before. A log of creek temperature from before a mine proposal is evidence nobody can reconstruct afterward.

00:16:16.177 --> 00:16:24.849
<v Casey>Evidence that a proponent's consultants will call too thin. A small dataset doesn't just need to be right. It needs to survive someone paid to dismiss it.

00:16:25.374 --> 00:16:33.694
<v Riley>Which is the argument for the written protocol and the outside check. A dataset that's been audited by a university lab is hard to wave away.

00:16:33.874 --> 00:16:34.986
<v Casey>Harder. Not impossible.

00:16:35.165 --> 00:16:36.125
<v Riley>So where do we land?

00:16:36.438 --> 00:16:55.926
<v Casey>Small works when the protocol is written down, the data is public, someone outside the project can check it, and the work is tied to an institution that outlasts any one volunteer. Small fails when it's a passion project with no handover, defended as charm. Most real projects sit somewhere between, and the hard part is telling which you are before year five.

00:16:56.583 --> 00:17:17.311
<v Riley>It's workable more often than you allow. A season of clean data on a river nobody measures beats zero, and the next step is small. One site, one sensor, one written protocol with a named backup person, raw readings posted publicly, then a university researcher or the provincial fisheries ministry asked whether they'd accept the series.

00:17:17.924 --> 00:17:33.748
<v Casey>And whether the community whose river it is has agreed to any of it. I still think most of these projects die at year five. If you try something like this near Horsefly, Williams Lake or Quesnel, or know of a group already doing it, tell us at feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:17:34.049 --> 00:17:35.529
<v Riley>Enjoy the rest of your Sunday.

00:17:35.839 --> 00:17:37.655
<v Casey>And may your first reading be boring.